WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:05.490
The incomparable Jackie Chan has been thrilling
audiences for over four decades with his singular

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:09.260
combination of insane martial arts skills
and great comedic timing.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:13.660
The other quality that sets Chan apart from
his peers is that he does his own stunts,

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:17.980
no matter how dangerous, cheating death virtually
every time he makes a movie.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:22.660
Here are a few scenes that posed grave danger
to the life and limbs of one of the greatest

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:24.500
action stars of all time.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:26.120
Drunken Master

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:32.100
Snake in the Eagle's Shadow was one of Chan's
first lead roles back in 1978, introducing

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:36.020
his distinct comic touch and distinguishing
him from the great Bruce Lee.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.829
The movie also featured rising star Hwang
Jang-Lee, the "king of the leg fighters,"

00:00:40.829 --> 00:00:42.740
whose powerful kicks have become legend.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.520
Jang-Lee and Chan's onscreen chemistry was
so good that Jang-Lee was cast as the villain

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:49.739
in Chan's follow-up, Drunken Master.

00:00:49.739 --> 00:00:54.339
While shooting Snake, Jang-Lee had accidentally
kicked out one of Chan's teeth, but Chan had

00:00:54.339 --> 00:00:56.329
apparently failed to learn his lesson.

00:00:56.329 --> 00:01:00.769
He suffered one of his first major on-set
injuries when one of Lee's thundering kicks

00:01:00.769 --> 00:01:05.040
connected with his brow bone, breaking it
and nearly causing him to lose an eye.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:10.320
In a 2014 interview, Hwang Jang-Lee was asked
to rank his top five kung-fu fighters — and

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.980
he didn't hesitate to rank Jackie Chan #1.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:14.380
Project A

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:20.040
In 1983's Project A, Hong Kong police team
up with the Coast Guard to fight pirates at

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:24.620
the turn of the 20th century, and it's pure
slapstick comedy by way of blinding martial

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:26.500
arts and insane stunt work.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:30.920
One of the most dangerous things Chan ever
did appears in the finished movie, and it

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:35.340
may have been the first scene to give audiences
the notion that Chan may be superhuman.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:40.020
In the scene, Chan's character Ma dangles
from the face of a 60-foot clock tower — but

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:41.320
things don't go well.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:55.680
After losing his grip and falling six stories
through a couple of awnings, Chan lands directly

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:56.440
on his head.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:00.260
Chan injured his spine during the stunt, but
the camera doesn't cut away.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:04.340
Instead, extras drag him to his feet, and
he continues the scene.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:05.540
Police Story

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:10.860
The Police Story series has been particularly
hazardous to Chan, starting with the 1985

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:11.660
original.

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:16.060
At the climax of a prolonged brawl inside
a shopping mall, Chan jumps from a railing

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:18.200
onto a pole covered in Christmas lights.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.960
The glass was fake, so Chan at least wasn't
sliced to ribbons.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:32.600
But the lights had heated up the pole, causing
him to sustain serious burns on his hands.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:36.980
The hard landing also dislocated Chan's pelvis
and injured two vertebrae.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:41.200
At least the stunt looked great in the finished
film, because there was no way they were getting

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:41.980
a second take.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:43.800
Armour of God

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:49.200
It was a completely routine shot that resulted
in Chan's closest brush with mortality.

00:02:49.209 --> 00:02:54.040
While shooting the 1986 film Armour of God
in Yugoslavia, Chan had to jump from the wall

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:58.260
of a castle to a nearby tree branch, a maneuver
that he'd normally be able to pull off in

00:02:58.260 --> 00:02:58.760
his sleep.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:03.439
Instead, he came crashing down onto the rocks
below, cracking his skull and sending a piece

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:07.660
of it into his brain, resulting in doctors
having to put a metal plate in his head.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:11.800
It seems like the kind of thing that might
put an action star out of commission for good,

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:15.900
but the near-fatal injury simply became the
highlight of Armour of God's outtake reel

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:20.080
— and over the next two decades, Chan would
continue to risk it all for the enjoyment

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:21.320
of his millions of fans.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:24.000
Armour of God II: Operation Condor

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:29.840
The second film in the Armour of God series,
released in 1991, sees Chan in Indiana Jones

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:34.620
mode, chasing after hidden treasure and dealing
with secret cults and hidden Nazi bases.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:39.460
It's packed to the brim with crazy fight sequences
and stunts, but it was another reasonably

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:41.460
simple task that nearly took Chan out.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:49.660
Clever editing hides Chan's terrible tumble
during filming.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:53.740
In the extended scene, Chan actually loses
his grip on the chain and plummets to the

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:54.240
ground.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.800
Even though he had dislocated his sternum,
he gives a thumbs up.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:01.560
It could have been worse — if he had landed
differently, he might have finished the job

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:04.700
he started on Project A and dislocated his
head.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:07.080
Police Story 3: Supercop

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:11.960
It's safe to say that most leading men have
never had their face rearranged by a helicopter,

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:14.260
but Jackie Chan is not most leading men.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:19.940
An incredibly perilous sequence in 1992's
Police Story 3: Supercop has Chan leap onto

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:24.070
a rope ladder dangling from a chopper, while
the pilot attempts to shake him off.

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:25.680
That wasn't even the dangerous part.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.220
When the helicopter later lands on a moving
train, all Chan has to do it avoid it as he

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:32.860
dangles from a rotating railway apparatus.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:44.240
A miss revealed in the outtakes resulted in
a fractured rib, broken shoulder, and dislocated

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:44.800
cheekbone.

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:48.860
Chan had to hang on with his good shoulder
while half-conscious, waiting for the crew

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:49.800
to cut him down.

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:52.220
It's easy to see how much worse it could have
been.

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:54.240
The Legend of Drunken Master

00:04:54.460 --> 00:05:00.340
It took 16 years for Drunken Master to receive
a sequel, 1994's The Legend of Drunken Master.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:04.720
Its climactic fight sequence is legendary,
but it was almost very different.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:08.840
Chan disagreed with the director's vision
for the final scene, and the conflict led

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:11.020
to him quitting the uncompleted production.

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:15.660
Chan himself took over directing duties…
and decided to repeatedly risk lighting himself

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:16.540
on fire.

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:29.560
What makes the sequence even more amazing
is that the take in the finished film is the

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:30.320
second one.

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:34.980
Chan felt that the first take didn't have
the proper rhythm, so he shot it again, because

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:36.380
he's apparently invincible.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:37.880
Who Am I?

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:40.090
1998's Who Am I?

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:44.700
places Chan in the middle of international
intrigue, working with a CIA agent to solve

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:50.090
a mystery surrounding missing scientists while
dodging hitmen in scenic locales — and of

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:53.080
course, performing one of the most insane
stunts ever put to film.

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:57.780
Rotterdam, in the Netherlands, is home to
Willemswerf, one of the world's more unique

00:05:57.780 --> 00:05:58.440
skyscrapers.

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:03.940
It features a sloping glass facade that runs
down one entire side of the 24-story building.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:08.560
In the climactic scene of the film, Chan slides
down the entire length of the facade.

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:17.320
Chan accomplished this stunt by literally
just sliding down the entire length of the

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:22.820
facade, unassisted, with no harnesses or other
special equipment, exactly as seen in the

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:23.320
film.

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:28.740
The slide takes nearly 45 seconds to complete
before Chan comes to a bone-jarring halt,

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:30.800
catching himself from flying over the edge.

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:35.020
It took him two weeks to work up the courage
to attempt what may be the most dangerous

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:36.460
stunt ever put to film.

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:38.140
New Police Story

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:43.090
Since the old Police Story series obviously
wanted him dead, Chan could have been forgiven

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:46.840
if he'd wanted to skip the 2004 reboot New
Police Story.

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:51.440
But in characteristic fashion, he threw himself
into the film's kinetic action scenes with

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:51.940
gusto.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.360
In one particularly terrifying scene, Chan
pursues a gang member who distracts him from

00:06:56.380 --> 00:06:59.740
the chase by gunning down the driver of a
loaded double-decker bus.

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:04.480
Chan leaps onto the roof of the out-of-control
bus, which proceeds to cause massive property

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:07.200
damage while the hazards come at him fast
and furious.

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:18.440
It's easy to spot about a hundred things that
could have gone wrong during the filming of

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:22.820
this sequence, any one of which could have
resulted in Chan literally losing his head.

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:27.280
But if it weren't crazy dangerous to shoot,
it wouldn't really be a Jackie Chan movie.

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:28.460
Thanks for watching!

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.470
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:33.690
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

